```ADD_PAPER
2502.07044v1
This paper extends the classical CES production function by integrating AGI-driven labor alongside traditional human labor, providing a quantitative framework to simulate the economic ramifications of AI–driven labor displacement. Through comprehensive numerical experiments, the authors analyze how AGI serves as both a substitute and a complement to human labor, exploring shifts in wage structures, total factor productivity, and overall income distribution. Detailed simulation results assess the conditions under which human labor is marginalized, emphasizing the model’s sensitivity to AGI capital accumulation and its feedback on economic growth. Importantly, the study outlines potential policy interventions—such as redistributive mechanisms and social contract renegotiation—to mitigate adverse labor market outcomes. These insights lay the groundwork for integrating advanced prompt engineering and reasoning techniques to refine future simulation models for measuring and mitigating AI’s transformative impacts on labor and shared prosperity.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
