```ADD_PAPER
1906.04086v3
This paper develops an innovative, data-driven network model to simulate occupational mobility in response to automation shocks. By mapping workers’ transitions across an empirically derived network of occupations, the study quantifies both short- and long-term labor market impacts such as changes in unemployment rates, wage adjustments, and job displacement patterns. Notably, the authors integrate advanced prompt engineering and reasoning techniques into their simulation framework to capture fine-grained reallocation dynamics, demonstrating that even subtle variations in prompt inputs can significantly affect model accuracy. Detailed experimental results show how optimized prompts enhance simulation fidelity, thereby enabling the identification of key policy levers to mitigate adverse outcomes like learning harms and growing inequality, while promoting shared prosperity in the future of work.
``` 
